# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Tool write-ups (description field) for Finding Common Ground. Keyed by tool media id."""
DESCRIPTIONS = {
"M1.S1.T1.C1.2D1": (
"The student looks down on Sameeksha's main room, a rectangle 12 ft wide and 16 ft long drawn on a faint one-foot grid. A 60 second clock counts down at the top. Under the room is a row of tile-side buttons from 1 ft to 16 ft, and beside the room is a found list that starts empty. There is one timed round, so the student races the clock and nobody else.\n\n"
"The student taps a side, say 3 ft, and the tool lays square tiles over the floor row by row from the top left corner. A side that fits covers the floor with no gap and no overlap, the tiles turn green, and a counter shows how many were used. A side that does not fit leaves bare floor along an edge, shaded red with the width of the leftover strip written on it. A 3 ft tile fits the 12 ft side in four tiles but leaves a 1 ft strip along the 16 ft side, and an 8 ft tile fits the 16 ft side in two tiles but leaves a 4 ft strip along the 12 ft side. Before the tiles are laid the student may tap Fit or Will not fit as a guess, and the guess is marked once the tiles land.\n\n"
"Each size that fits goes into the found list with its tile count. The student collects 1 ft (192 tiles), 2 ft (48 tiles) and 4 ft (12 tiles) and watches the count fall as the tile grows. Sameeksha wants as few tiles as possible and a whole number of feet, so the task is to find every size that fits and then choose the one she should buy. When the clock reaches zero the tool shows the sizes found, any fitting size that was missed, and the size the student chose to buy. It does not state a rule or give the number a name yet.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: does the tile fit exactly across the 12 ft side, and exactly along the 16 ft side? Then: a tile fits only if its side divides both 12 and 16 with nothing left over. Then: take the sides that divide 12 and test each one against 16. Last, the fitting sizes are laid one after another and the largest is marked. Through doing, the student learns that a tile fits only when its side goes into both lengths exactly, that more than one size can fit, and that the real question is which fitting size is the largest."
),
"M1.S2.T2.C5.2D1": (
"Two number cards stand side by side at the top, each with an empty row beneath it for factor chips. A middle strip below them waits for chips that belong to both numbers, with an HCF slot at its right end. At the bottom is a rectangle with a square-side slider. The first pair is 12 and 16.\n\n"
"The student first predicts which numbers will turn out to be common factors. Then the student taps candidate divisors for each card. A tap on a number that does not divide the card is shaken out and the remainder is shown, for example 16 divided by 3 leaves 1. A factor that was left out is flagged with a division check such as 24 divided by 8 equals 3. The row for 12 fills with 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12 and the row for 16 with 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16. The student drags a chip that appears in both rows into the middle strip, where its two copies glide together. A chip that divides only one number is sent back with the failed division shown. The strip ends with 1, 2 and 4, and the HCF slot lights up only when the greatest chip in the strip, 4, is placed in it.\n\n"
"Then the student moves the slider to lay square tiles of that side on the 12 ft by 16 ft rectangle. Sides 1, 2 and 4 cover it with no gap, and 4 needs the fewest tiles, 12. A side such as 8 leaves a strip uncovered, so the picture agrees with the strip of chips. The student sees that the HCF is also the side of the biggest square that tiles the rectangle, and that every common factor in these pairs divides the HCF.\n\n"
"Six pairs follow: 12 and 16 (HCF 4), 18 and 24 (HCF 6), 20 and 30 (HCF 10), 21 and 28 (HCF 7), 15 and 28 (HCF 1) and 36 and 48 (HCF 12). On 15 and 28 the strip holds only the chip 1, so two numbers can share nothing but 1. Hints ask which numbers divide the first number exactly, then suggest writing both rows and looking for chips in both, then ask the student to test the biggest chip in the strip against both numbers. Last, both rows are shown with the shared chips highlighted and the greatest marked, and a new pair follows. The student learns that a common factor must divide both numbers and that the greatest of them is the HCF."
),
"M1.S9.T9.C21.2D1": (
"A single number card sits alone on a blank canvas, and the job is to keep breaking it up until nothing more can be broken. A switch at the top changes the canvas between a factor tree and a division ladder with a vertical bar. A product line along the bottom collects the prime leaves, and a Check button waits beside it.\n\n"
"In tree mode the student first predicts whether different splits of the same number will end with the same primes. Then the student taps a card and types two factors whose product is that number, and two new cards hang below it. A prime leaf turns green and stops. A leaf that is still composite stays orange with a suggested split. The first card is 90, split twice. In the first tree 90 becomes 3 times 30, then 30 becomes 2 times 15 and 15 becomes 3 times 5. In the second tree 90 becomes 2 times 45, then 45 becomes 3 times 15 and 15 becomes 3 times 5. The two trees end with the same primes, 2, 3, 3 and 5, and the tool lines the two sets of leaves up side by side so the student sees only the order has changed.\n\n"
"In ladder mode the student writes a prime on the left of the bar and the quotient below it, then repeats until the number left is prime. For 105 the ladder is 105 divided by 3 is 35, 35 divided by 5 is 7, and 7 is prime, so 105 is 3 times 5 times 7. For 30 it gives 2 times 3 times 5. The cards continue with 1200 by division, which takes six divisions and ends at 5, giving four 2s, one 3 and two 5s, and with 72 by division, which gives three 2s and two 3s. Then 225 is split by a tree into two 3s and two 5s. The last card is 13, which is already prime, so it is its own factorisation, and a try at 1 times 13 is stopped with a note that 1 is not a prime and the split has got nowhere.\n\n"
"Check multiplies the leaves and shows the product beside the number, for example 2 times 3 times 3 times 5 equals 90. Hints ask whether the number is divisible by 2, 3 or 5, then suggest dividing by the smallest prime that goes in and carrying the quotient down, then say to keep dividing until the number left is prime. Last, the ladder is shown to the end with the primes multiplied back, and a new number follows. The student learns that every route reaches the same primes, that the division method records them compactly, and that a prime is its own factorisation."
),
"M1.S11.T11.C26.2D1": (
"The prime factorisation of a number is laid out as a row of coloured prime tiles. On the right a sorted factor list waits to grow, with a counter for the number of factors and a Check button below it. The first card is 225, so the tiles read 3, 3, 5 and 5.\n\n"
"Before building anything the student predicts how many factors 225 will have. Then the student selects tiles, and the product of the selected tiles appears and can be added to the list in order. One tile gives 3 and 5. Two tiles give 3 times 3 equals 9, 5 times 5 equals 25 and 3 times 5 equals 15, and choosing the other 3 with the other 5 makes 15 again, so that product is greyed with a note that it is already in the list. Three tiles give 45 and 75, and all four give 225. Selecting no tile gives 1, which the student adds at the end. The list reads 1, 3, 5, 9, 15, 25, 45, 75, 225 and the counter shows 9. The tiles left unselected multiply to the partner factor, so picking 3, 3 and 5 shows 45 with partner 5.\n\n"
"A gap in the sorted list hints at a missing factor, for example no 15 between 9 and 25. Check then divides the number by each candidate to confirm that no factor is missing and none is repeated. Because every product comes from the tiles, a number that is not a factor can never be built. The cards are 225 (9 factors), 90 (12 factors), 105 (8 factors), 132 (12 factors), 360 (24 factors) and 840 (32 factors). On 840 the tiles are 2, 2, 2, 3, 5 and 7, so the student works by the number of tiles used to stay organised, builds 28 from 2, 2 and 7 with partner 30, and finds that 27 cannot be built because there is only one 3 tile.\n\n"
"Hints ask what the primes in the factorisation are, then suggest combining the primes one at a time, then two, then three. Then: skip a product that is already in the list, and finish with 1. Last, the full list is shown with the tiles that make each factor, and a new number follows. The student learns that every factor is a subpart of the prime factorisation, so taking the primes one, two, three and more at a time lists all of them without missing any."
),
"M1.S15.T15.C41.2D1": (
"A table fills the screen with one row for each prime and one column for each number, a Min column on the right, and a product line underneath. The first card is 30 and 72, so the number columns read 2 times 3 times 5 and 2 times 2 times 2 times 3 times 3, and each cell shows how many times that prime occurs.\n\n"
"The student first predicts how many copies of each prime the HCF can hold. 30 has one 2 and 72 has three 2s, so the HCF can have only one 2. Then the student fills the Min column, choosing the smaller count in each row. For 3 the counts are 1 and 2, so the Min is 1. The prime 5 is in 30 but not in 72, so its row is crossed out automatically with a short reason. The product line builds 2 times 3, and the HCF is 6. A count taken from the larger number is flagged and the smaller count is highlighted.\n\n"
"Finally the tool checks the answer by dividing each number by the HCF: 30 divided by 6 is 5 and 72 divided by 6 is 12, both whole. The six cards are 30 and 72 (HCF 6), 225 and 750 (HCF 75, where 3 has counts 2 and 1 and 5 has counts 2 and 3), 24 and 180 (HCF 12), 42, 75 and 24 (HCF 3), 400 and 2500 (HCF 100) and 36, 60 and 84 (HCF 12). The extra number button adds a third column, as in the last card, where a prime must be in all three numbers to stay. On 42, 75 and 24 only the prime 3 survives, because each of 2, 5 and 7 is missing from at least one number. For 400 and 2500 the student splits both numbers first, and the minimum counts of 2 and of 5 are both 2, so the HCF is 2 times 2 times 5 times 5, which is 100.\n\n"
"Hints ask which primes occur in every number, then suggest choosing the smaller count for each shared prime, then multiplying the primes with their minimum counts. Last, the table shows the minimum counts and the product, and a new card follows. The student learns that the HCF takes each prime shared by all the numbers the minimum number of times, and that this works for two numbers or for more."
),
"M2.S17.T17.C48.2D1": (
"Two rows of cloth strips lie along a long ruler marked in centimetres, Anshu's row in one colour above the ruler and Guna's in another below it. Each row starts empty, with a button to add one strip of that row's length. An LCM slot sits at the right of the ruler. The first card gives Anshu strips of 6 cm and Guna strips of 8 cm.\n\n"
"The student first predicts whether the two rows will ever end at the same mark. Then the student adds strips one at a time. Each strip joins the end of the row and the running total is written at its right edge: 6, 12, 18, 24 for Anshu and 8, 16, 24 for Guna. A marker line drops from every total to the ruler. Where only one row ends, the other row's gap is shown, for example Anshu at 18 and Guna at 16. When both rows end at the same mark, that mark lights up on both. On this card it first lights at 24 cm, after four strips of 6 and three strips of 8.\n\n"
"The student names the shortest length where both rows end together, and the LCM slot lights up only for the first common mark. A longer common length, like 48, is accepted as a common multiple but not as the lowest. The student can keep adding strips and sees 48 and then 72 light up, so common lengths never stop and no largest one exists. The multiple lists are written beside the rows to check against. The cards are 6 and 8 (24 cm), 4 and 10 (20 cm), 9 and 12 (36 cm), 5 and 7 (35 cm), 8 and 12 (24 cm) and 6 and 15 (30 cm). On 5 and 7 the first match is 5 times 7, but on 8 and 12 it comes at 24, well before 8 times 12.\n\n"
"Hints ask where the first row ends after one strip, two strips, three strips, then suggest writing the totals for each row and looking for a number in both lists, then taking the smallest number in both lists. Last, both lists are shown with the shared totals highlighted and the smallest marked, and a new pair follows. The student learns that strips of one length only reach multiples of that length, that the first common mark is the LCM, and that the LCM is the lowest common multiple, not the biggest."
),
"M2.S21.T21.C58.2D1": (
"Each of two numbers is already broken into a row of prime tiles at the top of the screen, and an empty build tray waits underneath. Beside every prime sits a counter showing how many copies of it each number has. The first card is 14 and 35, so the rows read 2, 7 and 5, 7.\n\n"
"The student predicts the LCM first, then builds it. For each prime the student compares the copies in the two rows and drags the larger count into the tray. For 14 and 35 the counters show 2 with counts 1 and 0, 5 with counts 0 and 1, and 7 with counts 1 and 1, so the tray gets 2, 5 and 7. The tool multiplies the tray to 70. Check then tests whether the tray is divisible by both numbers, 70 divided by 14 is 5 and 70 divided by 35 is 2, and also removes one tile at a time to show that each removal breaks the divisibility. This is why nothing smaller works.\n\n"
"Wrong builds are answered with a reason. A tray holding only the shared prime 7 fails, and the missing primes 2 and 5 are highlighted. Taking the smaller count of a prime fails the check and names the number that does not divide, for example on 96 and 360 a tray with only three 2s gives 360, and 96 does not divide 360. An extra tile, such as a second 7, passes the check but is marked as not the lowest. The cards are 14 and 35 (LCM 70), 96 and 360 (LCM 1440, five 2s, two 3s and one 5), 12 and 18 (LCM 36), 20 and 30 (LCM 60), 45 and 75 (LCM 225) and 84 and 90 (LCM 1260).\n\n"
"Hints ask which primes make up each number, then ask which number has more copies of each prime. Then: put the larger count of each prime into the tray, including primes that are in only one number. Last, each prime's larger count is placed in the tray and multiplied to give the LCM, and a new pair follows. The student learns that a common multiple must contain the primes of both numbers, that the LCM takes the larger count of every prime, and that a prime in only one number still goes in."
),
"M3.S23.T23.C64.2D1": (
"A pair-type picker runs along the top with six choices: consecutive even numbers, consecutive odd numbers, consecutive numbers, co-prime pairs, one number a factor of the other, and two multiples of 3. Under it is a table with columns for the first number, the second number, the HCF, the LCM and the product, and a claim box for the student's general statement.\n\n"
"The student picks a type, for instance consecutive even numbers, and the generator makes a pair such as 4 and 6. The student fills in the HCF and LCM for each pair, here 2 and 12, then generates three or four more, such as 8 and 10 and 14 and 16, and looks at which column stays the same. Then the student writes a claim in the box, here that the HCF is 2 for every pair of consecutive even numbers, and tests it on pairs the tool generates, including one chosen to break it.\n\n"
"A claim that fails for a generated pair is answered with that pair as a counter-example. For consecutive odd numbers, a claim of HCF 2 meets 9 and 11, whose HCF is 1. A claim that holds for every pair shown is accepted, and the tool asks for a reason using common factors. A common factor of both numbers must also divide their difference. For consecutive even numbers the difference is 2 and both are even, so the HCF is 2. For consecutive odd numbers the difference is also 2, but 2 does not divide an odd number, so the HCF is 1. For consecutive numbers the difference is 1, so the HCF is 1. A reason that mentions the difference or the shared primes is marked complete.\n\n"
"The cards are consecutive even (HCF 2), consecutive odd (HCF 1), consecutive (HCF 1), co-prime pairs (HCF 1 and LCM the product, as for 9 and 10 giving 90), one number a factor of the other (HCF the smaller, LCM the larger, and with n and 5n the HCF is n and the LCM is 5n) and two multiples of 3 (the LCM of 6 and 9 is 18, always a multiple of 3). Hints ask the student to try three or four pairs and look at the HCFs, then to compare the columns, then to ask what a common factor must also divide. The student learns that a pattern is a statement that survives many examples and that it needs a reason that works for every pair."
),
"M3.S27.T27.C79.2D1": (
"A ladder with room for several rows is drawn down the screen, and the two numbers of the first pair are written on its top rung. Beside each row is a divisor box, below the last row sits a co-prime check light, and at the foot are an HCF slot and an LCM slot. The first pair is 84 and 180.\n\n"
"The student predicts what the divisors might give, then types a common divisor for the current row and the two quotients fill the next row. Dividing by 2 gives 42 and 90, dividing by 2 again gives 21 and 45, and dividing by 3 gives 7 and 15. The check light turns on when the last two numbers share no factor. A divisor that does not divide both numbers is rejected with the failed division shown, such as 84 divided by 5 leaving 4. A ladder stopped while the last row still shares a factor is flagged with that factor, for example 21 and 45 share 3.\n\n"
"The student then reads the HCF as the product of the divisors, 2 times 2 times 3 equals 12. Giving only the last row as the answer is flagged, because the answer is the product of the divisors. For the LCM the divisors are multiplied with the last row, 12 times 7 times 15 equals 1260, and the tool shows 84 as 12 times 7 and 180 as 12 times 15 inside that product, so it holds both numbers with nothing extra. The student can divide by any common factor, not only a prime, so 300 and 150 can be divided by 50 at once and then by 3, and 630 and 770 by 10 and then by 7.\n\n"
"The six pairs are 84 and 180 (HCF 12, LCM 1260), 300 and 150 (HCF 150, LCM 300), 630 and 770 (HCF 70, LCM 6930), 90 and 150 (HCF 30, LCM 450), 84 and 132 (HCF 12, LCM 924) and 56 and 84 (HCF 28, LCM 168). Hints ask which number divides both exactly, then suggest dividing both by a common factor and checking whether the new row still shares one. Then: keep going until the last two numbers share nothing, then multiply the divisors for the HCF. Last, the divisors and the last row are multiplied for the HCF and LCM, and a new pair follows. The student learns that one ladder gives both answers and that any common divisor works."
),
"M3.S29.T29.C86.2D1": (
"A balance scale stands in the middle of the screen with HCF times LCM on the left pan and the product of the two numbers on the right. Above it sit two number cards, and under it a prime split view, with a switch for three numbers.\n\n"
"The student enters or picks two numbers, say 105 and 95, and splits each into primes: 3, 5, 7 and 5, 19. The prime split view colours each prime by where it goes. The prime 5 is in both numbers, so one copy goes to the HCF and one to the LCM. The primes 3, 7 and 19 are in only one number, so they go to the LCM. The student fills in the HCF 5 and the LCM 1995, then presses Weigh. HCF times LCM is 5 times 1995, which is 9975, and the product is 105 times 95, also 9975, so the balance stays level.\n\n"
"A balance that tips shows which side is heavier and by how much, which helps if a value was wrong. A missing value is checked by multiplying back and comparing with the product. The cards are 105 and 95 (HCF 5, LCM 1995), 45 and 105 (HCF 15, LCM 315), 275 and 352 (HCF 11, LCM 8800), 222 and 370 (HCF 74, LCM 1110), a missing LCM when the HCF is 6 and the numbers are 12 and 18 (LCM 36, since 12 times 18 is 216 and 216 divided by 6 is 36), and three numbers, 4, 6 and 10.\n\n"
"In the last card the switch for three numbers is on. The HCF is 2 and the LCM is 60, so HCF times LCM is 120 but the product is 240, and the balance tips. The tool shows the prime 2, which has counts 2, 1 and 1. The minimum gives one 2 to the HCF and the maximum gives two 2s to the LCM, but the middle count of 1 goes nowhere. Hints ask for the prime factorisation of each number first, then to write the HCF and LCM and multiply them, then to compare with the product. Last, the HCF and LCM are multiplied and set beside the product, and for three numbers the two sides are shown to differ. The student learns that for two numbers each prime's smaller count goes to the HCF and its larger count to the LCM, so together they use exactly the primes of the product, and that this fails for three."
),
"M3.S31.T31.C95.2D1": (
"A short story card fills the top of the screen, with two big buttons below it, HCF and LCM, a prime factorisation workspace, an answer box and a story check line. The student has to decide what kind of story it is before doing any sums.\n\n"
"The student reads the story and decides whether it is about splitting into equal parts, which means the HCF, or about repeating events lining up, which means the LCM. A wrong choice is explained by naming the cue in the story, such as equal parts or meeting again. After the choice the student factorises the numbers in the workspace and takes minimum counts for an HCF or maximum counts for an LCM. The answer is typed with its unit, and the story check reads it back in the story: 105 cows would pass through 3 gates as 35 each, through 5 gates as 21 each and through 7 gates as 15 each. An answer that is right but has no unit is asked to be restated with the unit.\n\n"
"The six cards are different in kind. In blue stars, row one repeats every 6 stars and row two every 4, both are blue at the 4th star, and the LCM 12 gives the 16th star as the next meeting. In the prime-form question the two big buttons give way to Yes and No, and the workspace lines up the prime counts. The student answers whether 5 times 7 times 11 times 11 is a multiple, then a factor, of 5 times 7 times 7 times 11 times 2. Both answers are No: a multiple would need the two 7s and the 2, and a factor cannot have two 11s when the other has one. In the prime-form HCF and LCM, 3 times 3 times 5 times 7 times 7 and 12 times 7 times 11 give 21 and 97020. In HCF 1 and LCM 66, the numbers share no prime, so the student splits 2, 3 and 11 between them, for example 6 and 11. In cows, the LCM of 3, 5 and 7 is 105, and the next, 210, is over 200. In the 12 by 18 by 36 box, cubes of 6, 3 and 2 cm fit, but not 9 or 4.\n\n"
"Hints ask whether the story is cutting into equal parts or waiting for things to line up, then say that equal parts means HCF and lining up means LCM. Then: write the prime factorisations and take minimum or maximum counts. Last, the choice is named, the value is computed from the primes and read back in the story, and a new story follows. The student learns to read the cue in a story and choose the HCF or the LCM."
),
}
